b/id #edb/ref 17592186370862} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9cec83-4385-583c-a89c-4555fba3af7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370863} {:answer/id #uuid "2c71fcad-cfa7-5a0b-b267-6f77c29ecb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370864} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c6d885-533e-5717-9a05-16c9518eb9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c6d885-533e-5717-9a05-16c9518eb9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370865} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e57de48-558b-52c6-bdda-b1f60df5f00f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b717856-6a11-5857-bbda-def4fc991b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36792" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(2v)=2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0aaf960-68e6-52a2-aed5-3e8cfc5c518e" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Line Segment Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050563 :topic/math-academy-id 1379 :topic/title "Measures of Segments"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b33ab888-c6b6-52ed-84f4-07c7c8548d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104525} {:answer/id #uuid "b820000d-d1e6-5d9e-bbd3-08a921c57649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 